WEBVTT

00:51.280 --> 00:55.120
Okay, well good morning and welcome to

00:55.620 --> 00:59.200
our administration and finance committee meeting on March

00:59.700 --> 01:05.040
the 10th, 2026. I'll call on

01:06.080 --> 01:10.240
Tim Brubacher to perform the roll call

01:10.740 --> 01:14.240
again.

01:14.640 --> 01:17.920
Thank you. Regional Chair

01:18.420 --> 01:21.240
Redman is here. Present. Councillor Craig, Councillor Erb,

01:21.740 --> 01:25.020
Councillor Foxton, Councillor Gowing, Present. Chair Harris. Present.

01:25.520 --> 01:28.460
Councillor Huinink. Is here. Councillor James. Present. Councillor Liggett. Present. Councillor McCabe. Present.

01:28.960 --> 01:32.420
Councillor Nowak. Councillor Rodrigues. He is around the corner. There he is. Councillor Salonen.

01:32.920 --> 01:36.620
Councillor Shantz. Present. Councillor Vrbanovic. And Councillor

01:36.700 --> 01:40.196
Wolf. Thank you, Chair. Okay,

01:40.696 --> 01:44.620
I would like to acknowledge that the land on which we

01:44.780 --> 01:48.780
gather today is the traditional territory of the Haudenosaunee, Anishinaabe and Chonnonton peoples.

01:50.150 --> 01:53.510
We recognise the enduring presence of Indigenous peoples on this land

01:54.010 --> 01:57.390
and we honour their historic and

01:57.890 --> 02:01.110
ongoing contributions to our region. Are there

02:01.610 --> 02:06.350
any declarations of conflict of interest? Seeing none, there are no

02:06.850 --> 02:07.270
presentations,

02:07.510 --> 02:11.350
there are no

02:11.850 --> 02:15.510
delegations. I believe we have consent agenda item matters and we'd

02:16.390 --> 02:19.690
like to remove some. Councillor James. Thank you through you, Chair.

02:20.190 --> 02:23.770
Yes, I wanted to pull the consent agenda item regarding the investment position. I'm just

02:24.490 --> 02:27.850
noting that staff noted in the report that while our sinking funds operate under the

02:28.350 --> 02:31.690
requirements of the Municipal Act, there's currently no formal framework or policy for how actuarial

02:32.190 --> 02:36.050
surpluses within those reserves are managed. They specifically said there's no formal

02:36.550 --> 02:40.370
policy. So I'm

02:40.870 --> 02:45.280
just wondering if staff could undertake some analysis of

02:45.780 --> 02:49.160
that sinking fund and report back with

02:49.660 --> 02:53.840
possibly a recommended framework or policy, something that I think

02:54.340 --> 02:58.320
in terms of our fiscal responsibilities

02:58.820 --> 03:02.560
and going forward,

03:03.060 --> 03:08.000
having something, a framework and analysis, would be

03:08.500 --> 03:11.720
beneficial to us and future

03:12.220 --> 03:15.640
councils. I'll let our CFO

03:16.140 --> 03:19.820
chime in. Thanks for the question. And through

03:20.320 --> 03:24.073
the chair, we did identify that, that the practice for these surpluses is to leave

03:24.573 --> 03:28.260
it in the fund in the event that there are investment shortfalls in the

03:28.760 --> 03:30.660
future. But I agree, we can more formalize that, do a bit more analysis on

03:31.160 --> 03:34.380
that. And what I would recommend is we'll

03:34.880 --> 03:38.740
ensure this is included in the reserve fund analysis we're

03:39.240 --> 03:42.380
planning for later in the year where we look at all our reserves and look

03:42.880 --> 03:46.710
at what the appropriate level and balances. So we'll include that in here, talk about

03:46.790 --> 03:51.470
what those principles are and the risks of setting that at

03:51.970 --> 03:54.047
whatever level the analysis tells us. Okay, so just to confirm, so fall, so kind

03:54.547 --> 03:58.030
of before the next kind of budget

03:58.530 --> 04:01.950
cycle. Yes. Okay, thank you. We'll continue

04:02.450 --> 04:03.190
to entertain questions on this matter. Councillor Gowing.

04:06.550 --> 04:10.710
Thanks through you, Chair. So I was actually going

04:10.870 --> 04:14.470
to pull this

04:14.970 --> 04:18.290
this morning as well. Can you tell me on the general account or

04:18.790 --> 04:22.690
the general investment funds, how does the region's

04:23.190 --> 04:26.330
current liquidity level and investment ladder compare to peer municipalities?

04:26.830 --> 04:30.610
And what opportunities exist to optimize the returns while

04:31.730 --> 04:34.850
maintaining the prudent investment policy? Through the chair. So one of the

04:35.350 --> 04:41.330
ways we measure is through the municipal benchmarking initiative

04:41.830 --> 04:45.810
that we presented to council Council a few months ago where

04:46.130 --> 04:49.730
we identified different comparator groups and looking

04:49.810 --> 04:53.170
within that you can see that we

04:53.670 --> 04:56.850
are right in kind of

04:57.350 --> 05:01.250
the average or the norm around where

05:01.730 --> 05:03.570
those returns are. However,

05:03.970 --> 05:07.690
we are,

05:08.190 --> 05:11.970
we do mention in the report that we are looking at ways to improve those

05:12.470 --> 05:15.760
returns and there has been developments, it was a few years ago where the

05:16.260 --> 05:21.040
provincial government is allowing us to implement

05:21.540 --> 05:25.680
something called the prudent investor rule. So there's certain requirements

05:26.180 --> 05:29.640
we have to do to

05:30.140 --> 05:33.720
fulfill that. And we are working on a report to council to

05:34.220 --> 05:37.160
discuss that possibility,

05:37.240 --> 05:40.600
which can, it would increase the risk we take, but it would also,

05:41.100 --> 05:44.410
there's that risk return, we would be able to improve those returns. And so staff

05:44.910 --> 05:48.170
have been working on that. We've done some initial studies and

05:48.670 --> 05:51.503
we will be bringing that forward to council later this year. Okay, just a quick

05:52.003 --> 05:55.970
follow up chair. Yeah, go ahead. So yeah, because I did, I looked

05:56.470 --> 05:59.850
at our numbers and we and I talked this morning briefly about it. So I

06:00.350 --> 06:04.330
think if we look at that more closely because I'm glad that you

06:04.830 --> 06:06.970
gave me the information on where we're heading and what we're looking at. But if

06:07.470 --> 06:11.710
we look at other regions. We're a little bit behind

06:12.210 --> 06:15.590
on them from the research that I have done, but I think

06:16.090 --> 06:19.470
moving forward, if we look at our peers within Ontario and how they're structured,

06:20.030 --> 06:23.430
I think based on what the numbers I ran, we left

06:23.930 --> 06:27.190
about 2.8 million on the table when we could have had some better returns following

06:27.690 --> 06:31.510
their structure. So I just, I'm looking forward to seeing what you guys

06:32.010 --> 06:35.550
can come up with. Yeah,

06:36.050 --> 06:40.390
and just to follow up, my understanding is when this

06:40.890 --> 06:43.874
new direction came out from the province a few years ago,

06:43.946 --> 06:47.710
we did discuss, council did discuss that,

06:48.210 --> 06:51.416
but that was early days. So now we have some

06:51.916 --> 06:55.150
data from other municipalities that moved to this

06:55.650 --> 06:59.070
prudent investor standard. So we'll be able

06:59.570 --> 07:03.990
to bring that as well to the table and help to make the decision.

07:05.230 --> 07:09.270
I see no further questions on

07:09.770 --> 07:15.190
that topic. It was received for information. We'll move to our

07:15.690 --> 07:19.510
regular agenda item 7-1 strategic priority, equitable services and

07:20.010 --> 07:23.630
opportunities. This is the first item for regular business. We have the 2025 accessibility

07:24.030 --> 07:27.310
status report. I'll call upon our accessibility lead, Haley Gienow-McConnell,

07:27.790 --> 07:31.290
to provide a presentation. Colleagues, the report is for information. Good morning.

07:31.790 --> 07:35.730
Good morning. Thanks for having me here this

07:35.810 --> 07:38.970
morning and a special warm welcome to our new CAO. It's a pleasure to meet

07:39.470 --> 07:42.370
you. As mentioned, my name's Hailey and I'm here on behalf of the region's reconciliation,

07:42.870 --> 07:47.010
equity, diversity, and inclusion team, or the REDI team, as I'll refer

07:47.570 --> 07:50.930
to them for the rest of the conversation.

07:51.430 --> 07:56.290
And I'm presenting a status update on accessibility at

07:56.790 --> 08:00.450
the region. So this status update is

08:00.950 --> 08:04.540
both a legislated requirement under the Accessibility, Frontiers with

08:05.040 --> 08:07.240
Disabilities Act, which is kind of the boring part. But it's also an opportunity to

08:07.740 --> 08:10.940
let council and the community know what we've been up to

08:11.440 --> 08:15.900
when it comes to accessibility, which I think

08:16.400 --> 08:19.620
is the more fun part. Thanks. So we'll continue on

08:20.120 --> 08:23.420
slide two. Yep. So specifically today, I'm going to spend

08:23.920 --> 08:27.740
some time introducing myself and the role that I play as the

08:28.240 --> 08:31.660
region's accessibility lead. I'm also going to review with you the region's AODA compliance requirements

08:32.160 --> 08:33.140
and how we are meeting them. And we are meeting them. So that's great news.

08:33.819 --> 08:37.059
I'll be

08:37.559 --> 08:41.019
sharing some highlights from the region's accessibility status report.

08:41.259 --> 08:45.699
So that was shared with you

08:46.199 --> 08:50.059
through eScribe, including some milestones that we've achieved through our multi-year accessibility plan.

08:50.559 --> 08:54.339
The multi-year accessibility plan, its duration is from 2023 to 2027. So next year we'll

08:54.839 --> 08:58.059
be updating that plan. And finally, I'm going to discuss our planned approach for accessibility

08:58.139 --> 09:01.579
planning as we look forward to developing our next multi-year accessibility

09:01.659 --> 09:04.939
plan and really carving out this role and enhancing accessibility at the region generally.

09:06.200 --> 09:09.640
Next slide, please. So first off, it's important that council and the community

09:10.140 --> 09:13.680
know who is leading accessibility work at the region. So that

09:14.180 --> 09:17.520
would be me. My name is Haley. In December of 2024, this esteemed council

09:18.020 --> 09:20.720
approved funding for the creation of a full-time dedicated role to accessibility, and I'm honoured

09:21.220 --> 09:25.720
to have been selected for that role. I began working with the region in

09:26.220 --> 09:30.120
August of 2025, and that's a little bit of important context because

09:30.620 --> 09:33.000
a lot of the things I'm speaking about in today's report, there's a legacy in

09:33.500 --> 09:36.990
history that predates me, so I'll do my best to speak to that.

09:37.140 --> 09:40.420
But that's yeah, that's just a little background.

09:40.920 --> 09:44.460
So to this role I bring, I think importantly,

09:44.960 --> 09:48.420
lived experience with disability. I myself am a person with disability. My husband and daughter

09:48.920 --> 09:52.580
both live with disabilities as well. And I'm fluent in American Sign

09:53.080 --> 09:56.140
Language as my husband is a deaf ASL user. So shout out to the deaf

09:56.640 --> 09:59.980
community in the region. I also have an academic background.

10:00.480 --> 10:03.860
I hold a PhD in history and disability studies, which I know is kind

10:03.940 --> 10:07.660
of a bit of a nerd alert, but I've conducted research and published extensively

10:08.160 --> 10:11.840
on disability and I kind of feel like that's my

10:12.340 --> 10:15.546
former life that laid the groundwork for the work that I do

10:16.046 --> 10:19.160
now. So post-academic life, I gained a wealth of experience serving a wide range of

10:19.660 --> 10:24.320
organisations in a consultant role. So different industries and organisations, including many

10:24.820 --> 10:28.400
municipalities across Canada, working as an accessibility consultant. And in fact,

10:28.560 --> 10:31.660
previously I supported the region doing consulting work on accessibility. So I

10:32.160 --> 10:35.920
guess I didn't scare you away at that point. In terms of approach, over the

10:36.420 --> 10:39.240
course of my career, I have developed a person-centered, equity-oriented,

10:39.740 --> 10:43.540
and strategic approach to accessibility. Approach to accessibility, and I think you'll see

10:44.040 --> 10:47.180
that philosophy underpins much of what I'm going to be talking about today. So next

10:47.680 --> 10:51.460
slide, please. So that's a little bit about me, but more importantly, let's focus

10:51.960 --> 10:53.892
on the role. So what is the region's accessibility lead? Well, my work and portfolio

10:54.392 --> 10:56.300
includes helping the region maintain legislative compliance, as I said, you know, less interesting,

10:56.800 --> 11:00.220
but still a crucial part of the role. And I'll

11:00.720 --> 11:04.060
speak more on that in a moment, but it also importantly includes supporting departments across

11:04.560 --> 11:07.760
the organisation on their accessibility journeys. And so this could look like so

11:08.260 --> 11:11.800
sometimes it's ad hoc advising. People will come to me and ask for support and

11:12.300 --> 11:15.680
advice to ensure that they're carrying out their work in

11:16.160 --> 11:19.920
a way that's accessible, or it looks like longer term projects

11:20.420 --> 11:24.040
where I'm embedded in the project work as the accessibility lead and

11:24.540 --> 11:28.600
ensuring that that is built in from, you know, start to finish and project execution.

11:29.100 --> 11:32.840
The role also includes developing programs and

11:33.340 --> 11:36.920
resources, so this could be internal and external programs that

11:37.420 --> 11:40.500
support staff at the region to to have their needs met as

11:41.000 --> 11:44.180
employees with disabilities, but also to help them fulfill their roles in ways

11:44.680 --> 11:48.260
that promote accessibility throughout the work that they do. It also includes training. And again,

11:48.760 --> 11:51.420
this could be training for individuals to help support each other and their colleagues who

11:51.920 --> 11:55.300
have disabilities, or training for individuals to ensure that they're carrying out

11:55.800 --> 11:59.620
their roles in ways that serve the

12:00.120 --> 12:02.720
diversity of our community. And then I think most importantly, it's also about building relationships

12:03.220 --> 12:06.740
between the region and disability communities. So not just as an employee, but as

12:07.240 --> 12:09.740
a person with lived experience who has a connection to that community.

12:10.370 --> 12:13.930
I think that's really important and I

12:14.430 --> 12:17.236
take great pride and honour in that. I also want to be clear that my

12:17.736 --> 12:21.290
work applies to both internal, so HR and corporate spaces at the region and public

12:21.790 --> 12:25.570
facing work, so regional services and the public spaces. So it encompasses

12:26.070 --> 12:27.850
all of those things. Next slide, please. So my role lives within the ready team,

12:28.350 --> 12:31.130
as I mentioned at the region, but it does exist to support the entire organisation.

12:31.630 --> 12:35.690
And I really think of this role as supporting the pillars

12:36.190 --> 12:38.750
of growing with care and that strategy. And in particular, prioritizing accessibility at the region

12:39.250 --> 12:42.670
ensures that we're fulfilling the goals under equitable services and opportunities,

12:43.170 --> 12:46.350
as well as becoming a resilient and future ready organisation. So with that kind of

12:46.850 --> 12:50.390
mindset, I have frequently said that I'm

12:50.890 --> 12:54.890
the accessibility leader. I'm not the accessibility doer. And what

12:55.390 --> 12:58.310
that means is that we all have a responsibility at the region and a role

12:58.810 --> 13:02.390
to play in creating

13:02.710 --> 13:06.110
and enhancing and maintaining a culture of accessibility and ensuring that

13:06.610 --> 13:10.050
accessibility is embedded in everything that that we do.

13:10.550 --> 13:14.290
Next slide, please. So one of the ways that we're accountable to our commitment

13:14.790 --> 13:17.485
to accessibility is through our legislated responsibilities, and that's under the AODA. So those requirements

13:17.985 --> 13:22.090
include meeting the five integrated accessibility standards regulations in the AODA. That sounds like

13:22.590 --> 13:27.610
a lot of jargon, but at a high level, these standards

13:28.110 --> 13:31.770
relate to employment, information and communication, transportation, the design of programs and services, and public

13:32.270 --> 13:35.850
spaces. And there are specific regulations about how we execute those things. Kind of

13:36.350 --> 13:40.370
in a technical way. Other requirements

13:41.850 --> 13:45.530
we have is convening and consulting with an

13:46.030 --> 13:49.850
accessibility advisory committee. In our case, this is the Grand River Accessibility Advisory Committee on

13:50.350 --> 13:53.850
which I serve as the staff representative and have been privileged

13:54.350 --> 13:58.050
to develop a great relationship with them over the past seven months.

13:58.550 --> 14:02.930
Compliance also looks like developing and

14:03.430 --> 14:05.978
achieving our goals set forth in our multi-year accessibility plan, and I'll speak more to

14:06.478 --> 14:09.790
that in a few moments. And then finally completing an annual status report, which confirms

14:10.290 --> 14:13.710
that we've met our requirements under the AODA, which we

14:14.210 --> 14:17.950
have, and which describes how we've done those things. So my presentation today

14:18.450 --> 14:22.110
is kind of confirmation that we have met those requirements

14:22.610 --> 14:25.750
and obligations, but also highlighting some of the things that we've achieved under our multi-year

14:25.830 --> 14:29.590
accessibility plan. So beyond that compliance piece and looking towards action.

14:30.090 --> 14:33.830
Next slide, please. So as I previously mentioned, the AODA standards

14:34.330 --> 14:37.430
speak to five key areas, and that's how our multi-year accessibility plan is

14:37.930 --> 14:41.390
organized. So we think through those five critical areas of service

14:41.630 --> 14:44.310
and spaces, and then we organise our goals and hoped for wishes to achieve accessibility

14:44.810 --> 14:47.350
over the length of our multi-year accessibility plan. Under the AODA, these plans run for

14:47.850 --> 14:49.550
five years, but at the region we tend to cycle through them every four years

14:50.050 --> 14:53.350
just to align them with new council years so that everybody kind of has a

14:53.850 --> 14:57.550
hand and a buy-in in those new plans. So I want to highlight some of

14:58.050 --> 15:02.230
the things that we've achieved recently under these five areas, and I

15:02.730 --> 15:06.290
just want to remind everybody this is

15:06.790 --> 15:10.240
a sampling. It's not exhaustive, so the report details many more achievements. And I

15:10.740 --> 15:12.120
also want to be clear that these are the things that we kind of set

15:12.620 --> 15:16.360
forth as priorities that predated my time at the region,

15:16.860 --> 15:20.440
and I look forward to really developing and enriching the portfolio as well.

15:20.940 --> 15:23.279
So this is the beginning and not the end of accessibility. So as I said,

15:23.779 --> 15:27.920
I'll give you a highlight for each of the five kind of key areas

15:28.880 --> 15:32.480
under the AODA. So for example, in terms

15:32.980 --> 15:36.440
of the design of public spaces, grants were

15:36.940 --> 15:40.500
awarded through the Enabling Accessibility Fund for improvements to the physical space at

15:41.000 --> 15:43.300
Sunnyside. And I'd like to highlight that you can see with a lot of these

15:43.800 --> 15:46.406
achievements, they marry a lot of the, they overlap. There's kind of intersectional work.

15:46.906 --> 15:49.340
So yes, these were physical improvements to the space, but Sunnyside is a regional service

15:49.740 --> 15:53.060
that we provide. So in that way, we've improved both the

15:53.560 --> 15:56.380
design of public services and the design of public spaces. Similarly,

15:57.100 --> 16:00.900
in terms of customer service,

16:01.400 --> 16:05.180
as we know, waste has kind of undergone a revamp and our assisted waste collection

16:05.260 --> 16:08.540
program for people who have physical limitations to getting their trash

16:09.040 --> 16:11.980
to the curb, as well as who may produce additional waste due to medical equipment

16:12.480 --> 16:15.220
they require, and therefore they may require exemptions for the amount of waste that they

16:15.720 --> 16:19.980
produce and put to their curb. That program is now in full swing,

16:20.480 --> 16:25.060
and so we're pleased to offer that as a service to people with disabilities in

16:25.560 --> 16:29.193
the region needing support in order to access those services. We've also

16:29.693 --> 16:32.300
had an increase in accessible housing units and inventory. So, you know, there's a number

16:32.800 --> 16:34.020
of housing projects, um, along the way. And once again, this sort of shows the

16:34.520 --> 16:37.640
inter sectional perspective of accessibility. So we

16:38.140 --> 16:41.640
know that there's a housing crisis in the region.

16:42.140 --> 16:45.400
And so building new inventory in general helps the general community, but then within that

16:45.900 --> 16:48.960
inventory, ensuring there are accessible units, therefore makes it more inclusive for people both who

16:49.460 --> 16:53.520
are low income, have disabilities, newcomers, etc. In terms

16:54.020 --> 16:58.200
of information and communications, we are working on a

16:58.700 --> 17:00.720
website revitalization project. So this is a critical touch point for members of the community

17:01.220 --> 17:05.250
to understand what we do, how we do it, and to get information and

17:05.750 --> 17:08.050
access to services. And as a part of that project, I'm serving on that project,

17:08.550 --> 17:11.130
we've been doing some accessibility user testing.

17:11.530 --> 17:15.250
So members of the community with disabilities playing around

17:15.750 --> 17:18.730
with the new website. It's one thing to say,

17:19.230 --> 17:23.130
oh, we've met technical accessibility guidelines, but if the user experience isn't there, it's not

17:23.290 --> 17:25.770
worth much. And so we're working hard to do that. And the launch date for

17:26.270 --> 17:29.960
the new website revitalization project is in later spring. In terms

17:30.460 --> 17:34.320
of employment, one of the big things that I'm

17:34.820 --> 17:38.280
really pleased about is that we've done a review of and are working on

17:38.780 --> 17:42.400
updates to the hiring and accommodations policies and

17:42.480 --> 17:45.813
process. So this is ongoing, so it hasn't been implemented yet,

17:46.313 --> 17:49.600
but I'm working with HR and Abilities Management to ensure that the policy not

17:50.100 --> 17:54.080
only is effective in delivering effective accommodations for people with disabilities who work for

17:54.580 --> 17:58.200
the region, but also the language in it and the execution of

17:58.700 --> 18:02.460
it is inclusive, that it that it's person centred,

18:02.960 --> 18:06.340
that it puts a positive focus on looking really at accommodations as

18:06.840 --> 18:10.780
a way to achieve meaningful employment and have people have the

18:11.280 --> 18:14.820
supports they need to thrive and work versus saying, you know, we're reducing barriers and

18:15.320 --> 18:18.700
making it seem like it's coming from a place of negativism.

18:18.860 --> 18:22.140
So pleased to see that. We're also going to

18:22.380 --> 18:25.820
accompany those new policies with some additional training to ensure that

18:26.320 --> 18:29.820
managers and supervisors feel supported, that they know how to have those conversations about accessibility

18:30.320 --> 18:33.720
and accommodations in a way, again, that is respectful, dignified, person-centered. And inclusive. And finally,

18:34.280 --> 18:37.600
in terms of transportation, lots going on there, but one

18:38.100 --> 18:41.400
of the things that's well underway is mobility plus modernization. So our paratransit

18:41.900 --> 18:45.400
services are vital services to people with disabilities in the community

18:45.480 --> 18:48.160
and really looking forward to improvements through both the technology itself and the user experience

18:48.660 --> 18:51.640
to access those services. We've been working hard to, you know, on getting the project

18:52.140 --> 18:54.600
launched. But in addition to that, we've also been working really hard, and I would

18:55.100 --> 18:58.830
like to give GRT great credit in developing a really positive relationship with

18:59.330 --> 19:02.990
the Accessibility Advisory Committee. They've really taken an

19:03.490 --> 19:06.430
approach to consulting early and often and taking that feedback with, for example,

19:07.230 --> 19:10.350
one platform or station improvement, carrying that forward to the next project,

19:10.850 --> 19:14.450
bringing that to the committee. And I can say that the committee has

19:14.950 --> 19:17.235
spoken very highly of this process many times, indicating, wow, the thing we said last

19:17.735 --> 19:20.830
time they proactively did this time, and now we get to take it to the

19:21.330 --> 19:24.600
next level and deepen our feedback. So that's been really wonderful. Next slide, please.

19:25.100 --> 19:29.000
So today's presentation is a reflection of what we've achieved

19:29.240 --> 19:33.160
in the recent past when it comes to accessibility,

19:33.660 --> 19:37.320
and it includes the ways that the region

19:37.820 --> 19:41.280
has kind of taken care to achieve compliance under the AODA. But I like to

19:41.780 --> 19:44.480
think that the creation of the accessibility lead role is a demonstration of the region's

19:44.980 --> 19:49.320
commitment to move beyond a compliance mindset. So this isn't off the side of

19:49.400 --> 19:52.900
our desk work. This is high priority work. I and

19:53.400 --> 19:56.900
my team, as well as the departments that I support

19:57.400 --> 20:00.860
and that have been really welcoming to me in my new role in the

20:01.360 --> 20:05.100
region, were invested in meaningful, sustainable, and community-driven change. So we're focusing on this by

20:05.600 --> 20:09.300
A, as I've said several times, moving beyond compliance.

20:09.460 --> 20:12.060
So the actions that we do are not just, okay, here's what the AODA says

20:12.560 --> 20:15.406
we have to do, but they're being driven by need as identified by community and

20:15.906 --> 20:19.310
staff. And that's really come about through consulting early and often. So, as I said,

20:19.810 --> 20:22.350
there's been recent success kind of using that iterative approach. Sometimes we come to community

20:22.850 --> 20:25.710
and just say, this is what we're working on. We're not sure where it's going

20:26.210 --> 20:29.670
yet. An FYI, other times it's a deeper level of consultation. What do

20:30.170 --> 20:33.790
you need? What does this look like to you? We're also developing an internal

20:34.290 --> 20:37.790
accessibility path forward. So this is in development now, something that hopefully

20:38.290 --> 20:41.510
we can share with you in the coming days.

20:42.010 --> 20:45.140
This is an internal document, so it's separate from the multi-year access accessibility plan that's

20:45.640 --> 20:49.540
presented to the wider community about what we're committing

20:50.040 --> 20:53.140
to. And this is really going to be, we see it as

20:53.640 --> 20:57.420
a guidance document that sets the vision and priorities for accessibility so that

20:57.920 --> 21:01.420
it anchors our work and we kind of really understand what we're

21:01.920 --> 21:05.420
doing and how we do it. And then finally, we're working on capacity building

21:05.920 --> 21:09.820
across the organisation. So as I mentioned, this is training

21:10.320 --> 21:13.700
work, advisory work, and supporting communities of practice. So some

21:14.200 --> 21:17.000
of you may be familiar with our ready action partnership. So So I work in

21:17.500 --> 21:21.160
the REDI team, but there are people across the organization who are doing reconciliation,

21:21.660 --> 21:25.020
equity, diversity, and inclusion work who come together and talk about what

21:25.520 --> 21:28.480
are we achieving, what are the gaps, what are your learnings, how can we support

21:28.980 --> 21:32.520
each other in uplifting this work, as well as a community engagement

21:33.020 --> 21:35.920
community of practice. So this is an area, again, where all of us are invested

21:36.420 --> 21:39.680
in making sure that we're delivering equitable and inclusive services and community driven services.

21:40.180 --> 21:41.826
And so what does that look like? How do we do that effectively? How do

21:42.326 --> 21:46.010
we reach stakeholders in ways that are accessible and equitable and then translate

21:46.510 --> 21:49.770
that back into action, including those actions for the accessibility portfolio. So I just want

21:50.270 --> 21:53.810
to sum up to say, I'm the one standing up here today, but it

21:54.310 --> 21:58.250
is far from me alone. I am just one resource that has set

21:58.750 --> 22:01.690
in motion a greater vision for accessibility. Today I've mostly spoken of, as I said,

22:02.190 --> 22:05.810
legacy, but I hope that I've also indicated what we're doing to plan for the

22:06.310 --> 22:09.730
future. And yeah, it's just a distinct honour today to represent the region and our

22:10.230 --> 22:13.880
commitments towards accessibility. So I'll do my best to answer

22:14.380 --> 22:18.133
questions. I also have colleagues here supporting me if there are areas that

22:18.633 --> 22:22.306
they're more equipped to speak to. So thank you. Great. Thank you

22:22.806 --> 22:25.160
for your presentation. We do have some questions. Councillor James.

22:27.239 --> 22:32.360
Thank you through you, Chair. Haley, thank you so

22:32.860 --> 22:34.320
much for your presentation and to the entire team for what you're doing. And you

22:34.820 --> 22:36.420
said it very eloquently, you don't do this alone and this work cannot be done

22:36.920 --> 22:39.080
alone and shouldn't be. I do have two questions, Chair Harris. And Haley and the

22:39.580 --> 22:42.680
team, just on your action items. So I went through them in actions to see

22:43.180 --> 22:46.400
around the accessible units. And I was just going through 82 Wilson, there's 11 accessible

22:46.900 --> 22:50.840
units. And then Lang's has 5%, no, not 5%, but they

22:51.340 --> 22:54.400
have, I think you said 29 accessible units. How are the accessible units determined?

23:05.050 --> 23:08.890
What is the kind of that threshold, I guess,

23:09.930 --> 23:13.770
in terms of our housing and what's

23:14.270 --> 23:17.690
required? Yeah, through you, Chir, that's a great question. I'm going to invite my

23:18.190 --> 23:22.290
colleague, Amy, to speak

23:22.790 --> 23:26.970
to that. Those determinations predated me coming

23:27.470 --> 23:30.170
to the region, so if Amy would like to join.

23:31.360 --> 23:34.280
Good morning, Council. Through the chair, thank you for the question, Councillor James. So this

23:34.780 --> 23:37.880
was a percentage of the build. So when we did the design

23:38.380 --> 23:42.280
and we did the application for

23:42.780 --> 23:46.200
funding, it does require that a certain percentage of the units are

23:46.700 --> 23:49.880
accessible. And that's how it works out to those numbers.

23:50.380 --> 23:54.000
Okay, thank you. And that's kind of the provincially set standards.

23:54.500 --> 23:56.680
Okay. And then just my other question, when I went through the report, it said

23:57.180 --> 24:01.110
that, The committee, the GRAC committee, there's the terms of

24:01.610 --> 24:04.630
reference, but there's currently some restructuring happening. Can you speak to what that restructuring is?

24:05.130 --> 24:09.030
To a degree. So we're reviewing the terms of reference for the Grand

24:09.530 --> 24:13.030
River Accessibility Advisory Committee to ensure

24:13.530 --> 24:16.670
that it's continuing to meet the community's needs, but also individual area municipalities. So some

24:17.170 --> 24:20.110
municipalities are exploring what the best organisation for that is,

24:20.610 --> 24:24.100
but currently the committee is

24:24.600 --> 24:28.340
existing and operating in the form that we've always known it. So we're just being

24:28.740 --> 24:32.020
intentional about thinking through just because it has

24:32.340 --> 24:35.780
existed in a certain fashion in a certain organisation for,

24:35.860 --> 24:38.500
I believe, 19 years now. Okay, thank you. Councillor Foxon.

24:39.060 --> 24:42.900
Thank you. And through you, Chair, I had the privilege

24:43.400 --> 24:46.980
of sitting on GRAC for a number of years. And I don't know

24:47.060 --> 24:50.000
if this group realises how much GRAC does. Every municipality,

24:50.310 --> 24:53.270
Whatever they build, they bring to GRAC. GRAC comments on every single one. They have

24:53.770 --> 24:57.750
a team of people with disabilities that go out and

24:58.250 --> 25:01.350
check out all these facilities, including the ION. And then

25:01.850 --> 25:05.510
they came back with the document that blew Tom Galloway away, where he had

25:05.670 --> 25:09.510
to go and revisit the ION stations and

25:10.010 --> 25:13.310
see where this situation was, which was great because we can

25:13.810 --> 25:16.230
deal with things this way.

25:18.110 --> 25:22.550
It's a great group. I have one other question and

25:23.050 --> 25:27.270
it's actually for Neil. Do we still

25:27.770 --> 25:31.590
have the disability group for Mobility Plus?

25:32.090 --> 25:35.750
Yes, we do. Okay, so there is a secondary group that

25:36.250 --> 25:39.310
checks on Modility Plus that meets with Neil and his group to

25:39.810 --> 25:42.430
make sure we're maintaining this. Now you talked about the discussion, GraC and going forward.

25:42.590 --> 25:46.350
And I think this council needs to realise that GraC could

25:46.430 --> 25:49.960
possibly splinter. Right now it works well. The City of Kitchener offers space and staffing

25:50.460 --> 25:52.300
and all of the individuals bust there, but it looks like... I know the townships

25:52.800 --> 25:55.420
in the City of Kitchener will like to remain together. It looks like the region's

25:55.920 --> 25:57.460
looking to opt out with the City of Waterloo, but I'm not sure where that's

25:57.960 --> 26:01.860
going to. Cambridge has always been on their own and that's okay.

26:02.500 --> 26:06.820
We respect that. They have their own group and they've decided that from

26:07.320 --> 26:10.620
day one. But I think this group, as a council,

26:11.120 --> 26:15.080
should be aware of the situation right now. Because I

26:15.580 --> 26:17.840
know the CAOs have met and the townships and the city of Kitchener would like

26:18.160 --> 26:21.680
remain together. We would like

26:21.760 --> 26:25.640
it to remain whole with the region in Waterloo,

26:26.140 --> 26:28.960
but that'll be a regional decision in the city of Waterloo. Thank you.

26:29.680 --> 26:32.680
Okay. I think there was a question in there somewhere, but you did, to be

26:33.180 --> 26:36.920
fair. Councillor Huinink. Thank you, Haley, for your report

26:37.420 --> 26:41.130
and your presentation. It has a pleasure collaborating

26:41.630 --> 26:45.290
with you on a number of things. And thank you for your work to support

26:45.370 --> 26:49.290
staff and council and not only broadening

26:49.450 --> 26:52.970
our understandings of disability and accessibility,

26:53.370 --> 26:57.290
but also making sure that we implement it well.

26:57.690 --> 27:01.890
In reference to what Councillor

27:02.390 --> 27:05.980
Foxton has alluded to, there have been discussions about

27:06.480 --> 27:10.620
the future format of GRAC, which has been quite distressing to

27:11.120 --> 27:14.740
Kitchener members that I'm aware of. And so I'm

27:15.240 --> 27:19.180
wondering if prior to

27:19.680 --> 27:23.540
the next election cycle, you can bring back a report to inform

27:24.040 --> 27:27.340
council as to what future

27:27.840 --> 27:28.820
formats could look like.

27:30.590 --> 27:34.190
Yeah, thank you, Councillor Huinink, and through you, Chair. So maybe a point of clarification

27:34.690 --> 27:38.190
about GRAC, and thank you, Councillor Foxton, for bringing that up. So some municipalities are

27:38.270 --> 27:41.950
interested in exploring other opportunities. The region's stance is

27:42.450 --> 27:46.310
that we believe a joint committee is the best option to serve the community,

27:46.810 --> 27:50.430
and it is in interest of the committee

27:50.930 --> 27:54.390
itself. Committee members have expressed that that is their desire, and so we

27:54.890 --> 27:58.190
are willing to support whatever whatever looks like the best path forward. So I think

27:58.270 --> 28:01.950
just as a point of clarification, I think the

28:02.030 --> 28:05.590
language you used, the region was looking to opt out. We are not.

28:06.090 --> 28:09.590
We're looking to stay in and remain unified where possible. But of course, we can't.

28:10.090 --> 28:12.430
We don't have jurisdiction over the other municipalities decisions if they choose to pursue a

28:12.930 --> 28:16.590
different Avenue. So I'm being guarded about the way that I'm expressing it

28:16.910 --> 28:20.270
only because I actually don't have the full insights

28:20.770 --> 28:25.870
and I just know what our intentions are in conversations that we've had

28:26.370 --> 28:29.270
internally with the CAO that, yeah,

28:29.350 --> 28:32.710
so for council and community, a joint effort is the region's desire,

28:33.210 --> 28:36.670
hope, and intended path forward. And we'll do whatever we

28:37.170 --> 28:41.510
can to hopefully make that happen. Just a follow up, Haley, if I may.

28:42.010 --> 28:45.790
If Kitchener does decide to create their own advisory committee, are Kitchener residents

28:46.290 --> 28:48.930
still allowed to remain part of the regional GRAC if they so choose? Yeah,

28:49.430 --> 28:51.770
through you chair. So I don't want to speak to a hypothetical organization of any

28:52.270 --> 28:55.610
committee that we might have to organize. Again, that would be somewhat of a reactionary

28:56.170 --> 28:59.770
measure to decisions that other municipalities

28:59.930 --> 29:03.210
are making. And so we would hope to, if it comes to that,

29:03.710 --> 29:07.370
be as inclusive as possible. We are the region. We do represent

29:07.870 --> 29:10.890
all of the area municipalities. And so I don't see why there would be any

29:11.390 --> 29:14.090
barriers to any particular resident in their geographic location. But yeah,

29:14.410 --> 29:17.730
it would be speculation at this at this point,

29:18.230 --> 29:21.690
so I don't, that's as much as I'm able to say,

29:21.850 --> 29:25.450
but thank you. Thank you very much for your work and your diplomacy.

29:25.950 --> 29:28.970
Chair, thank you, Haley. Your energy is just wonderful to see, so thank you very

29:29.470 --> 29:33.330
much. And at 9:00 a.m. on a Tuesday, it's probably a variation on a theme,

29:33.830 --> 29:39.250
but just, I have friends who are members of the disabled community that have been

29:39.750 --> 29:41.930
on GRAC probably for the last 19 or 20 years, and they're very dedicated and

29:42.990 --> 29:46.590
seen that that work has moved the needle. So when you come

29:47.090 --> 29:51.150
back with a report, I'm wondering if you can

29:51.390 --> 29:54.430
tell people who may not be in the gallery but may be watching, we'll be

29:54.930 --> 29:59.150
able to tell them how they could become members of

29:59.650 --> 30:02.470
GRAC if other people would like to join again along with these sort of legacy

30:02.970 --> 30:07.310
people who continue to find it very gratifying to participate.

30:07.810 --> 30:11.110
Through you, Chair, I think that would be a wonderful

30:11.610 --> 30:15.190
idea and especially because we want Any work that we're

30:15.690 --> 30:19.630
doing with any future advisory bodies, including the Specialized Transit Service Committee

30:20.130 --> 30:23.950
that was previously mentioned, as well as through GRAC, we want it to

30:24.450 --> 30:26.590
reflect the diversity of the region. Diversity isn't just through disability identities, but, you know,

30:27.090 --> 30:30.790
gender, age, demographic, newcomers. And so I think that would be wonderful to have kind

30:30.950 --> 30:34.590
of an open invitation to welcome some fresh faces. Thanks for your

30:35.090 --> 30:38.150
leadership, Councillor McCabe. Thank you through you,

30:38.650 --> 30:41.750
Chair. Welcome to the region. And obviously you're really engaged and excited about

30:42.250 --> 30:47.800
the work you're doing. So appreciate

30:48.300 --> 30:52.000
the energy. Hopefully you'll have that level of energy the next time you're presenting.

30:52.500 --> 30:55.600
Just a couple, well, just a clarification to start with, the City of

30:56.100 --> 31:00.040
Waterloo is not pulling out of GRAC. We're very much engaged

31:00.540 --> 31:03.840
in the work that GRAC is doing, and in addition to a significant amount of

31:04.340 --> 31:06.720
work that we're doing at the area, with the area municipality, and with Amy Ross,

31:07.220 --> 31:10.240
who's our accessibility lead, who maybe you know through your work. But no, we're very

31:10.740 --> 31:14.630
much engaged and appreciate the work

31:15.130 --> 31:17.910
that and the role that we can play and contributions we can make jointly through

31:18.410 --> 31:22.150
GRAC. So just want to correct the record on

31:22.650 --> 31:25.510
that. We are not pulling away from that. But I wanted to ask,

31:25.590 --> 31:29.350
when it's talking about design of public spaces,

31:30.070 --> 31:33.670
I'm curious, and this may be too in the weeds, but I'm curious about

31:34.170 --> 31:37.440
when we're looking at renovating buildings or building new buildings, What's the best practice with

31:37.940 --> 31:41.840
respect to stairs? Because it just seems like they are such a barrier, but there's

31:42.400 --> 31:45.920
still things that are being built. And, you know, when just reflecting over

31:46.420 --> 31:51.920
the weekend when you see this sudden melt, right? Like, whenever there's

31:52.420 --> 31:56.480
a, like that kind of a slope or gradiation

31:56.880 --> 32:00.520
can help for lots and lots of people and lots and lots of reasons

32:01.020 --> 32:04.980
whether they have a disability disability or

32:05.480 --> 32:09.300
not. So just wondering if you could comment on that. I know it's

32:09.800 --> 32:12.780
really in the weeds.

32:13.280 --> 32:17.100
Yeah, through you, Jared, and thank you for your work as

32:17.600 --> 32:21.740
well and the City of Waterloo's work on that. We're really

32:22.240 --> 32:26.020
enjoying that partnership. Yes, there are standards for design of public spaces, design and

32:26.520 --> 32:29.780
construction in terms of, so when you're asking about best practices for stairs,

32:30.280 --> 32:34.220
are you indicating what the best practice is just to enter the building? So stairs

32:34.720 --> 32:36.870
versus ramps? Versus can you clarify that?

32:38.230 --> 32:41.310
I'm just like, why do we continue, like, are ramps,

32:41.810 --> 32:45.310
sorry, I said slopes, are ramps not, would they not be considered best

32:45.810 --> 32:48.150
practice versus stairs? You don't really have to go into it all. I just feel

32:48.650 --> 32:51.750
like it's just frustrating to me to see stairs continuing to be built when we

32:52.250 --> 32:55.750
know that things like universal design would provide, like of a ramp would provide much

32:56.250 --> 32:59.430
better access for people of all ages and also, you know, for snow

32:59.930 --> 33:03.080
melting and there wouldn't be, you know, stairs to clear. Build up

33:03.580 --> 33:08.200
and things like that? Yeah, through you chair, that's a great question.

33:08.700 --> 33:11.560
I haven't actually ever seen any discussion on the idea of doing away with stairs

33:11.640 --> 33:15.200
altogether. And I think that's something that I'd like

33:15.700 --> 33:18.200
to investigate. Okay, well, yeah, it's a great, great food for thought. But I appreciate

33:18.360 --> 33:20.213
as well that there is the idea, as you say, like this. This also shows

33:20.713 --> 33:24.040
the integrated work of accessibility, because, sure, we can build a great

33:24.540 --> 33:27.280
building, but if maintenance and facilities aren't up on snow clearing, then you can build

33:27.780 --> 33:30.480
an accessible building in design, but if it's blocked by snow, ice. Other barriers,

33:30.980 --> 33:33.200
or if it's not in good health and maintenance, so a path is heaving or

33:33.700 --> 33:34.880
cracking. So I think it speaks to a lot of the sort of wraparound supports

33:35.380 --> 33:39.080
and considerations that, you know, you can build the building, but if you just

33:39.160 --> 33:41.040
leave it as it is and don't have that kind of integrated holistic support to

33:41.540 --> 33:45.320
maintain the building and the infrastructure, you can lose accessibility as

33:45.879 --> 33:49.800
well. But that's, yeah, that's going to be my tossing and turning

33:50.300 --> 33:54.200
in bed at night. Why do we need stairs at all? I don't

33:54.700 --> 33:57.620
know. I hope you don't toss and turn too much.

33:58.010 --> 34:01.690
But yeah, I'll leave that with you to

34:02.190 --> 34:05.530
think about, because it just seems like something that we could address relatively

34:06.030 --> 34:08.810
easily. And then my other question is just about, and I don't know if it's

34:09.310 --> 34:13.130
really for you, but just about how are our accessibility requests

34:13.630 --> 34:16.810
integrated or are they integrated into the great deal of work

34:17.530 --> 34:20.570
that the chair and other members do around government advocacy?

34:21.070 --> 34:24.170
Do we include this in our requests?

34:25.530 --> 34:28.450
And just wondered how that looks like.

34:28.950 --> 34:32.570
Yeah, through you, Chair, I may actually direct Councillor

34:32.730 --> 34:36.490
Hunnig to speak on this point

34:36.650 --> 34:40.490
because I think they have some great insights on that, if I

34:40.990 --> 34:45.170
may. Thank you. I try to bring up accessibility in

34:45.670 --> 34:49.050
every conversation that I am at AMO for. One of the things that I'm particularly

34:49.690 --> 34:53.460
concerned about is I work from home versus work in

34:54.020 --> 34:57.780
on the premises policy and just making sure that

34:58.180 --> 35:01.140
that's flexible and accessible for anyone.

35:01.640 --> 35:05.220
And if there are other policies that

35:05.300 --> 35:08.820
need to be advocated to the province, I encourage staff

35:09.320 --> 35:13.340
and Council and members of community to let me know because I

35:13.840 --> 35:17.260
have no issue bringing them forward. Thank you. Okay, Councillor Foxton.

35:17.500 --> 35:20.700
Thank you, Chair. Just to respond

35:20.780 --> 35:24.660
to Councillor McCabe, I'm a right leg

35:25.160 --> 35:28.660
amputee. I cannot do ramps. Most people with canes or walking disabilities

35:29.160 --> 35:32.980
cannot do ramps. It either pitches them forward or pitches them back.

35:33.480 --> 35:39.180
So the only access we can

35:39.900 --> 35:43.820
use are elevators or stairs. So we must look at all avenues rather than

35:44.060 --> 35:47.096
considering Just one. Thank you. And also,

35:47.596 --> 35:50.550
I'm heartened to see that the region and Waterloo want to remain in GRAC.

35:51.050 --> 35:53.390
I think it's a great organization.

35:55.310 --> 35:59.230
I was honoured to serve on them the many years that I had served

35:59.730 --> 36:03.150
on them. And I hope that we can maintain this

36:03.650 --> 36:07.230
strong relationship because it is essential. And they're an amazing group.

36:07.390 --> 36:10.710
Thank you. Okay, that concludes all the questions we

36:11.210 --> 36:13.790
have. Thank you so much for your presentations and questions.

36:14.330 --> 36:19.370
From Council. That report was received for information. We're moving on to

36:19.870 --> 36:23.130
7-2 Strategic Priority, Resilient and Future Ready Organisation. We have a report on

36:23.630 --> 36:26.570
the proposed updates to the Procedural Bylaw. I'll call upon Regional Clerk Tim Brubacher to

36:27.070 --> 36:30.890
provide the introduction. Thank you, Chair. Council, on the

36:31.390 --> 36:35.690
agenda today, our series of housekeeping amendments were

36:36.190 --> 36:40.170
proposing to the Procedural Bylaw. The current bylaw was passed

36:40.670 --> 36:44.640
in 2022 and represented a significant change from the previous

36:45.140 --> 36:50.080
bylaw, was essentially rewritten to make the rules

36:50.580 --> 36:54.200
for committee and council to be the same. And so it was shortened,

36:54.360 --> 36:57.480
but we believe was an improvement over the previous

36:57.980 --> 37:02.160
one. However, throughout the term of council, our staff have maintained a

37:02.660 --> 37:06.040
list, pardon me, of potential changes to the bylaw

37:06.280 --> 37:09.640
just based on situations that have arisen

37:10.140 --> 37:12.730
throughout the term. Appendix A of the report lists each of the changes, and the

37:13.230 --> 37:16.860
rationale behind our recommendation. I'll highlight a couple of the changes.

37:16.940 --> 37:18.700
One is that delegations that register to speak on a report that's released on the

37:19.100 --> 37:22.260
addendum agenda will be given the full five minutes, even if they register after the

37:22.760 --> 37:26.220
deadline. As long as they register to speak an hour before the meeting. We ran

37:26.720 --> 37:30.940
